Post-Viral Sensory Dysfunction and Neuropathies
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Persistent post-viral numbness/tingling, Burning neuropathic pain, Preserved motor strength, Normal sensory nerve conduction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-041
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of agonizing, burning neuropathic pain in the bilateral lower extremities preventing sleep and ambulation, requiring urgent analgesic intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing an acute crisis of severe, burning pain in both feet and legs, resulting in a total inability to sleep or bear weight. Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out acute spinal cord pathology, confirm the neuropathic origin, and administer immediate targeted pain relief prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet feel like they are submerged in boiling water, the burning is agonizing, and even the bedsheets touching my skin makes me want to scream."
History of Present Illness: The patient recovered from a documented acute viral infection (influenza-like illness) approximately 6 weeks ago. The initial acute viral symptoms including fever and myalgia completely resolved. However, since then, the patient developed persistent, continuous altered sensation, including severe numbness, profound tingling, and intense formication. Over the last 48 hours, this has escalated into severe burning, shooting, and sharp neuropathic pain in a symmetrical bilateral 'stocking-glove' distribution affecting the distal extremities. The patient reports severe allodynia, where normally non-painful light touch provokes excruciating pain. They also note unexplainable profound sensory fatigue and hypersensitivity to bright lights or loud noises.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly distressed, actively keeping blankets off the lower legs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; severe sleep deprivation and acute psychological distress due to unremitting pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, hyper-vigilant regarding their lower extremities.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals profound hyperalgesia and allodynia over the bilateral feet and distal calves. Crucially, there is objective preservation of normal motor strength (5/5 power in ankle dorsiflexion/plantarflexion) and normal deep tendon reflexes at the knees and ankles. No focal spinal tenderness. Pulses are strongly palpable bilaterally. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Persistent post-viral numbness/tingling, Burning neuropathic pain, Preserved motor strength, and Normal sensory nerve conduction (presumed, pending formal neurology review).
Environmental Observations: Patient has discarded socks and blankets on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum doses of paracetamol and ibuprofen with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Sensory Neuropathy - Status: Exacerbated (Pain Crisis)
Differentials:

Guillain-Barré Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute preservation of motor strength and completely normal deep tendon reflexes (rather than rapidly progressive flaccid weakness and areflexia).

Acute Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical stocking-glove distribution rather than a distinct dermatomal sensory level, normal motor function, and intact bowel/bladder control.

Peripheral Arterial Ischaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Strongly palpable distal pulses, warm extremities, and bilateral symmetrical presentation rather than a cold, pulseless, unilateral limb.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a severe acute neuropathic pain crisis following a viral illness. The profound allodynia has caused extreme distress and sleep deprivation. Standard anti-inflammatories are entirely ineffective for this specific nerve pain pathology. An urgent on-site clinical assessment was strictly required to physically confirm the absence of motor weakness or areflexia (ruling out life-threatening Guillain-Barré Syndrome) and to immediately prescribe specific, potent neuromodulating agents (e.g., Pregabalin, Amitriptyline). Delaying this intervention would leave the patient in agonizing, untreated distress, fully satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a rapid-acting neuropathic agent immediately (e.g., Pregabalin 75mg stat, then daily) to dampen the hyperactive sensory nerves. Cease all NSAIDs as they offer no neuropathic benefit. Provide urgent referral for formal nerve conduction studies and neurology review via the regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Pregabalin exactly 2 hours before the desired bedtime, the patient will maximize the peak sedative and analgesic effect during the night; achieving a block of uninterrupted sleep will powerfully reinforce the daily use of this medication to break the pain-insomnia cycle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to titrate the neuropathic medication dosage.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops sudden, profound weakness in the legs (an inability to stand or lift the feet), a sudden inability to pass urine, or loss of bowel control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; formal nerve conduction studies are required to completely characterize the neuropathy.
Clinical Course: The virus has triggered an inflammatory response that left your sensory nerve endings hyper-excitable. This condition is chronic but often slowly improves over many months. The immediate goal is strictly symptom control, not a rapid cure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your nerves are currently misfiring, sending severe pain signals despite there being no actual tissue damage. However, if your legs suddenly become physically weak, or if you lose control of your bladder, you must call an ambulance immediately, as this indicates a completely different, life-threatening nerve condition.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Standard painkillers like ibuprofen only treat inflammation and tissue damage, which is why they have failed entirely. The new medication (Pregabalin) works directly in the brain and spinal cord to turn down the "volume" of these hyperactive nerve signals.
Actionable Steps: Take the new medication strictly at night, as it will cause drowsiness. Wear loose, frictionless clothing (like silk or satin) on your legs to minimize the severe burning provoked by touch.

